WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Both DNA strands carry complementary information.

2
00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:07.360
How does a cell choose which strand the polymerase reads?

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:13.140
Watch the same polymerase approach a stretch of duplex with no promoter marked.

4
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.460
It could face either way, so these base pairs alone give us no answer.

5
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:23.553
A promoter changes that.

6
00:00:24.713 --> 00:00:28.373
It is a DNA site where initiation machinery assembles.

7
00:00:29.573 --> 00:00:33.753
Its orientation places polymerase at a start site, facing downstream.

8
00:00:35.173 --> 00:00:37.213
The polymerase opens a short bubble.

9
00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:41.373
One strand enters the active site as the template.

10
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:46.373
RNA polymerase now matches RNA letters to the template.

11
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.973
It joins each new letter to the growing end.

12
00:00:51.153 --> 00:00:53.413
That growing end is the 3′ end.

13
00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:58.193
RNA therefore extends from 5′ to 3′.

14
00:00:58.193 --> 00:01:03.233
The polymerase moves along the template from 3′ to 5′.

15
00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:08.203
Suppose the promoter points the other way on the same duplex.

16
00:01:09.383 --> 00:01:13.123
Which strand will enter the active site, and which way will RNA grow?

17
00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.210
The whole arrangement reverses.

18
00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:27.210
The other DNA strand becomes the template, and the polymerase travels in the

19
00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.210
opposite direction.

20
00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:30.810
Chemistry has not changed.

21
00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:35.030
RNA still grows 5′ to 3′.

22
00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:40.930
Flipping the start's orientation changed both the strand read and the direction of

23
00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:41.930
travel.

24
00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.560
The DNA strand not used as the template is the coding strand.

25
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.040
Read in the same direction as RNA, its sequence matches the RNA.

26
00:01:53.380 --> 00:02:00.220
RNA uses U where DNA uses T. The RNA pairs with the template instead, so those

27
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.840
strands are complementary and antiparallel.

28
00:02:03.983 --> 00:02:08.083
Now remove the promoter and RNA, leaving only the duplex.

29
00:02:09.283 --> 00:02:11.743
The template label becomes uncertain again.

30
00:02:13.043 --> 00:02:18.763
An RNA product can restore the answer because it matches coding DNA and pairs with

31
00:02:18.763 --> 00:02:19.763
template DNA.

32
00:02:20.717 --> 00:02:24.657
DNA opens ahead of polymerase and closes behind it.

33
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:28.557
The growing RNA peels away through a separate path.

34
00:02:29.837 --> 00:02:34.517
Polymerase reads one strand while preserving the other strand's sequence in RNA.

35
00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.660
Use these checks on any sequence.

36
00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.980
Write both DNA strands 5′ to 3′.

37
00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:49.840
Match RNA to coding DNA after swapping U for T. Then confirm that RNA pairs

38
00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:51.380
antiparallel to the template.

39
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.080
Together, these checks give the direction of travel.

40
00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.280
The polymerase moves toward the template's 5′ end.

41
00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:05.133
This rule applies whenever RNA is copied from double-stranded DNA.

42
00:03:06.513 --> 00:03:10.513
Promoters vary, and different cells use different initiation proteins.

43
00:03:12.033 --> 00:03:17.333
Yet once a start and its direction are set, RNA grows by adding to its 3′ end.

44
00:03:18.953 --> 00:03:24.013
Antiparallel pairing then makes the polymerase read the template 3′ to 5′.
